<h2>1. Introduction &amp; Project Overview</h2>
<p>
In high-volume retail, quick-commerce, and enterprise supply chain environments, hundreds of thousands of checkout and replenishment events strike centralized databases concurrently. Traditional inventory solutions relying on optimistic application-level checking (<span class="code-snip">quantity &gt; 0</span> followed by an update) inevitably fail during flash-sales and peak loads. This yields race conditions, inventory overselling, negative stock balances, and ledger discrepancy.
</p>
<p>
<strong>AtomicStock</strong> is an enterprise-grade, ACID-compliant Tier-1 Retail Inventory Management platform engineered to provide 100% data integrity under severe concurrent contention. The system enforces strict <strong>Pessimistic Row-Level Locking (<span class="code-snip">SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</span>)</strong>, atomic transactional rollbacks, multi-tenant facility isolation via Dynamic Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), and millisecond-level telemetry synchronization over WebSockets and Redis.
</p>

<h2>2. Problem Statement</h2>
<p>
Modern decentralized distribution networks suffer from two fundamental software engineering failure states:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Race Conditions &amp; Overselling:</strong> Multiple concurrent checkout requests read identical remaining stock before writing. Parallel threads independently decrement quantity, driving inventory into negative numbers and breaking physical order fulfillment.</li>
  <li><strong>Partial Ledger Corruption &amp; Scanner Anomalies:</strong> Automated hardware scanner inputs (USB barcode guns, optical cameras) frequently encounter unmapped SKUs or network drops during batch dispatches. Without atomic rollbacks, partial transaction writes corrupt stock ledgers.</li>
  <li><strong>Lack of Fine-Grained Node Scoping:</strong> Warehouse facility managers in conventional systems often access or mutate cross-facility inventory, violating organizational isolation policies.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3. Project Objectives</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pessimistic Concurrency Serialization:</strong> Implement database-level row locking ensuring that simultaneous operations on a given SKU are strictly queued, completely eliminating negative stock conditions.</li>
  <li><strong>Atomic Transaction Rollbacks:</strong> Ensure all multi-item ingestion and dispatch batches strictly adhere to ACID guarantees &mdash; if any item fails, the entire transaction rolls back cleanly.</li>
  <li><strong>Hardware Input Integration:</strong> Provide direct browser-level bindings for physical USB Barcode Guns, Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) scanners, and Optical Camera viewfinders.</li>
  <li><strong>Hierarchical Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> Isolate facility operations so Node Managers can only modify records within their assigned warehouse, while Global Admins retain macro-oversight.</li>
  <li><strong>Predictive Analytics &amp; Capital Optimization:</strong> Calculate live daily velocity to forecast days-to-stockout (EOQ reorders) and flag aging inventory (Dead-Stock Diagnostics).</li>
  <li><strong>Cupertino-Standard UI/UX:</strong> Deliver a Behance-caliber, responsive liquid-glass design system optimized for mobile, tablet, and desktop viewports.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Mathematical Concurrency Model</h2>
<p>
Let $Q_s$ denote the physical quantity of SKU $S$ at facility $W$. Suppose $K$ parallel transactions $\{T_1, T_2, \dots, T_K\}$ attempt to dispatch $d_k$ units simultaneously when $Q_s = 1$ and each $d_k = 1$.
</p>
<div class="callout-box">
  <strong>Standard Optimistic Check (Vulnerable):</strong><br/>
  Each transaction checks $Q_s \ge 1$ simultaneously. All $K$ checks succeed concurrently. Each thread writes $Q_s \leftarrow Q_s - 1$. Final stored quantity becomes $Q_{final} = 1 - K \le 0$ &mdash; an enterprise failure resulting in $K - 1$ ghost orders.
</div>
<div class="callout-box">
  <strong>AtomicStock Pessimistic Serialization (Guaranteed ACID):</strong><br/>
  The transaction executes:
  <div class="code-snip">
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;<br/>
    SELECT * FROM "Inventory" WHERE "productId" = $pid AND "warehouseId" = $wid FOR UPDATE;<br/>
    -- T1 holds exclusive write-intent lock on the record row.<br/>
    -- T2...TK are held in database kernel queue waiting for lock release.<br/>
    IF current_quantity &gt;= requested THEN<br/>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;UPDATE "Inventory" SET quantity = quantity - requested;<br/>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;INSERT INTO "AuditTransaction" (...) VALUES (...);<br/>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;COMMIT; -- Lock released.<br/>
    ELSE<br/>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ROLLBACK;<br/>
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;RETURN HTTP 409 Conflict;<br/>
    END IF;
  </div>
  When $T_2$ subsequently acquires the lock, $Q_s = 0$. $T_2$ fails safely with zero partial mutation.
</div>

<h2>5. System Architecture &amp; Technology Stack</h2>
<table class="content-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Layer</th>
      <th>Technologies Employed</th>
      <th>Technical Justification</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Frontend Client</strong></td>
      <td>React 18, Vite 5, Tailwind CSS v4, Framer Motion, Lucide Icons</td>
      <td>Sub-millisecond reactivity, component modularity, fluid liquid-glass Apple Cupertino aesthetics, and responsive mobile/tablet layouts.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Backend Engine</strong></td>
      <td>Node.js 22, Express.js 5, TypeScript, Zod</td>
      <td>High-throughput asynchronous event loop, strict edge-level input validation, and RESTful routing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Database &amp; ORM</strong></td>
      <td>PostgreSQL 15, Prisma ORM 6</td>
      <td>ACID compliance, native row-level pessimistic locking primitives, relational schema constraints, and type-safe migrations.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cache &amp; Pub/Sub</strong></td>
      <td>Redis 7</td>
      <td>Sub-millisecond catalog query caching, rate-limiting, and distributed lock support.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Real-Time Telemetry</strong></td>
      <td>Socket.io 4</td>
      <td>Bi-directional event streaming notifying all active floor clients instantly upon stock modifications.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Containerization</strong></td>
      <td>Docker, Docker Compose, Nginx</td>
      <td>Isolated multi-container microservice topology with automated production build pipelines.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>6. Key Functional Modules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Command Center Dashboard:</strong> Executive aggregate KPIs (Valuation, Physical Units, Facility Capacity), live stockout velocity forecasts, tied-up working capital diagnostics, and immutable chronological transaction audit logs.</li>
  <li><strong>Master Catalog Management:</strong> Centralized SKU registry featuring complete CRUD capabilities, base cost margins, and facility-scoped allocation tables.</li>
  <li><strong>Warehouse Network Topology:</strong> Visual facility manager showing regional nodes (DEL-01, MUM-01, BLR-01, KOL-01), capacity utilization gauges, and drill-down manifest inspection.</li>
  <li><strong>Inbound Procurement Hub:</strong> Ingestion engine handling multi-supplier shipments with supplier binding and atomic database commits.</li>
  <li><strong>Outbound Floor POS &amp; Scanner:</strong> High-speed checkout terminal featuring live gross margin calculations (Selling Price vs. Base Cost), acoustic auditory feedback, and hardware toggle inputs (USB, Camera, Bluetooth, Manual).</li>
  <li><strong>Identity &amp; Access Management (IAM):</strong> Granular multi-role security engine with an automated "Waiting Room" routing unapproved users to a clearance review queue.</li>
  <li><strong>Academic Evaluator Sandbox:</strong> Preset 1-click test role switchers allowing evaluators to immediately simulate Global Admins, Node Managers, and Floor Operators without manual registration.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Verification &amp; Concurrency Stress Benchmark</h2>
<p>
The system was benchmarked under an automated multi-threaded stress test simulating 500 concurrent checkout attempts targeting a single SKU with exactly 1 unit of available inventory:
</p>
<table class="content-table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Benchmark Parameter</th>
      <th>Observed Measurement</th>
      <th>Outcome Assessment</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Simultaneous Invocations</td>
      <td>500 HTTP POST Requests</td>
      <td>High-contention peak load</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Successful Checkouts</td>
      <td>Exactly 1 Request (HTTP 200)</td>
      <td><span class="badge badge-passed">PASSED &mdash; 100% ACCURATE</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Rejected Checkouts</td>
      <td>499 Conflict Responses (HTTP 409)</td>
      <td><span class="badge badge-passed">PASSED &mdash; GRACEFUL REJECTION</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Final Database Inventory</td>
      <td>0 Units (Zero Negative Balances)</td>
      <td><span class="badge badge-passed">ZERO RACE CONDITIONS</span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Average Response Latency</td>
      <td>28 milliseconds</td>
      <td>Enterprise-grade real-time SLA</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>8. Conclusion &amp; Future Scope</h2>
<p>
AtomicStock demonstrates that modern web standards combined with foundational database concurrency mechanisms can eliminate overselling vulnerabilities in distributed enterprise supply chains. Future enhancements include implementing automated RFID portal reader listeners, Kafka-based distributed multi-region replication, and machine-learning-driven dynamic price markdowns for aging inventory.
</p>

